title: Writing in an unintegrated child, where the subscribee as such draws it, integrates it. Since 2026-11-14.
pid: 3a78f299-d464-4862-82f5-bc06fbc38a22
body: |2-
  Jeff reads Top's mail in his fork of Top's 'to Jeff', whose
  subscribee folder lists the messages he has neither filed nor hidden:
  his unread list (Ed's finding). Tested on two skgrepos, sender and
  reader, before the change: the reader's reply under a message forked
  it, and the redraw showed the clone with the reply in the message's
  place, since a view of one's own note follows one's own overrides.
  So the reply did not vanish for the reader. But the message still
  counted unread ('C2/' stayed), and no note contained the clone: it
  showed only by substitution, under a list the reader cannot write.
  Now the save files the member in the subscriber. Any edit at or below
  an unintegrated member, where an owned, editable subscriber's
  subscribee folder draws it (a reply, which forks it, or an edit of an
  owner's overrider shown there), appends that member to the
  subscriber's contains, by the ID the subscribee's list names, the
  original. The count falls by one, and the clone is drawn in its place
  under the subscriber, reply and all ('file_what_is_written_in.rs' in
  'server/from_text/'; 'docs/forks.org', "Writing in an unintegrated
  child integrates it").
  The save also moves the member's headline to the end of the
  subscriber's content in the saved view, as the user would have. It
  must: otherwise the rerender drew the filed member at its new place
  write-protected and empty, because the old occurrence, deeper, still
  held the node's one editability when the new one was visited in level
  order, and then vanished.
  Decided for Jeff, all reversible: the end of the subscriber's content,
  not the start; filing, not hiding (a hide would put the message in the
  folded hidden folder, and leave the reply in no note); and only for a
  subscriber the user owns and the save writes. Where the subscriber is
  another's, nothing is filed; that is the third party's case, under
  "Consequence: a fork made in such a view vanishes from it."
  What the review made me add: only a member the subscribee's list
  names is filed (a node the user placed under the subscribee as such is
  not its member); nothing is filed that the subscriber holds a fork of,
  which would be drawn twice; nothing is filed in a subscriber that
  changed since the buffer was drawn and is otherwise unedited, which
  would refuse the whole save as stale; the record takes
  'default_relRepo', so an owned private member is not named in a public
  section; and only content counts as written in, not a member's
  folders. Not done: a subscriber drawn write-protected, its editable
  occurrence elsewhere in the buffer, files nothing.
